Statements:
[
    "Generation creates initial synthetic "goldens" as raw QA pairs.",
    "These are the first, automatically generated examples.",
    "Filtration uses a critique model and a threshold to score the goldens and remove the bad ones.",
    "The threshold is default around 0.5 and can be tuned; in the example set it is 0.6.",
    "Filtration drops questions that do not meet generation standards.",
    "Filtration drops questions that could not safely be returned from an LLM.",
    "This step ensures the remaining set is higher quality before further work.",
    "Evolution takes the filtered goldens, not the raw ones.",
    "Evolution rewrites the filtered goldens with an LLM one or more times.",
    "The number of rewrites is controlled by num_evolution_times.",
    "Evolution increases complexity and realism.",
    "Evolution makes synthetic examples more like human-curated QAs.",
    "Evolved examples are more interesting, harder, and more realistic.",
    "Evolved examples better test real-world applications.",
    "The goal is to approach human quality.",
    "The target is about 80 to 90 percent of human quality.",
    "This involves a scalability and quality trade-off.",
    "Styling applies a consistent style to the input and output formats of the evolved goldens.",
    "Styling is configured in the evolution config.",
    "Styling makes examples uniform and realistic for downstream use.",
    "Styling improves readability.",
    "Styling helps meet format expectations.",
    "Styling makes evaluation easier.",
    "In short, many raw examples are generated first.",
    "Low-quality examples are then filtered out with a critique model and threshold.",
    "The survivors are evolved into more complex, realistic QA pairs.",
    "Their format is then styled for consistency and usability."
] 

